  doi: "10.48550/arXiv.2610.27951"
  url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27951"
  title: "infomeasure-rs: A High-Performance Rust Library for Information-Theoretic Measures and Estimators"
  year: 2026
  month: 10
  abstract: |
    Information theory has become a standard tool across the natural and social
    sciences, yet practical use is limited by estimator choice, fragmented
    software, reproducibility, and computational cost. We present
    infomeasure-rs, an open-source Rust library that brings a broad set of
    information-theoretic measures and estimators into one self-contained,
    type-safe package. It covers entropy, mutual information, conditional
    mutual information, transfer entropy, conditional transfer entropy,
    cross-entropy, and the Kullback-Leibler and Jensen-Shannon divergences, with
    discrete (plug-in and bias-corrected), kernel, ordinal, and
    nearest-neighbour estimators, including the Rényi and Tsallis families, and
    exposes local and global values and significance testing through a
    consistent trait interface. It ships as a single portable binary with no
    runtime dependency and is continuously validated against a reference
    implementation. On a single central processing unit (CPU) core, and even
    without its GPU or parallel paths, it is the fastest of the packages we
    compared across languages, matching the closest packages only for plug-in
    discrete entropy and substantially faster for mutual information, the
    conditional and transfer measures, and the kernel, ordinal, and
    generalised-entropy estimators. The optional GPU and multi-threaded paths
    accelerate selected continuous estimators further. The library is open
    source and, to our knowledge, the most complete information-theory package
    for Rust.
